<!doctype html><html lang="en-GB"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>Action recovery · Umicom Framework</title><link rel="stylesheet" href="../assets/site.css"><script defer src="../assets/site.js"></script>@UMICOM_SITE_ICONS@</head><body><a class="skip" href="#content">Skip to content</a><header class="top">@UMICOM_SITE_BRAND@<nav aria-label="Main navigation"><a href="index.html">Wiki</a><a href="../source-map.html">Source map</a><a href="../products.html">Applications</a></nav></header><main id="content"><h1>Keeping application state clear when an action fails</h1>
<p>A useful application explains what happened and protects the work already in progress. When Studio cannot finish reading a project folder, the existing project list should remain available. When Trader rejects an action, the interface should show the reason. When Desk cannot confirm that an application has stopped, it should keep track of that process rather than start another copy.</p>
<p>This guide follows those three situations through the shared Framework contracts. It introduces the terms, shows where the code lives, and provides an executable example that does not connect to a broker or stop a real application.</p>
<nav aria-label="Contents"><a href="#projects">Project discovery</a><a href="#feedback">Trading feedback</a><a href="#launch">Application lifecycle</a><a href="#example">Build the example</a><a href="#source">Source map</a></nav>
<h2 id="projects">1. Discover projects without publishing half a result</h2>
<p>A <strong>workspace</strong> is the folder you have opened for development. It may contain several projects. Framework keeps their names, locations and identifiers in a <em>workspace graph</em>. A project identifier lets a panel refer to the same project even when the panel is rebuilt.</p>
<p>Suppose the workspace contains Notes and a small reporting tool. Discovery checks its immediate child folders for <code>CMakeLists.txt</code>, <code>.umicom-root</code> or <code>application.umicom.yaml</code>. Finding a marker adds a project description; it does not execute the project or trust its code.</p>
<p>The graph prepares a private candidate list. It publishes that list only after the directory walk succeeds and the workspace has not changed. Think of this as preparing an edit before saving it: readers keep the previous complete version while the next one is being prepared.</p>
<pre><code>UmiStatus status = umi_workspace_graph_discover(graph);
if (status == UMI_STATUS_OK) {
    /* Read a fresh snapshot and update the project view. */
} else {
    /* Keep the current list and explain status to the user. */
}</code></pre><p>This fragment belongs inside a host that already owns an open graph. The complete temporary-folder exercise is in <code>tests/action_recovery/test_discovery.c</code>. Its capacity case prepares a nearly full project list, attempts discovery, checks that rejection left the list unchanged, then frees a slot and retries.</p>
<p>A <strong>revision</strong> is a counter used to identify a particular state. If another operation closes the workspace, changes its trust or edits its project list during discovery, the candidate belongs to an older revision. Framework returns <code>UMI_STATUS_INVALID_STATE</code> instead of writing over the newer state.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Call or result</th><th>Purpose and use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>umi_workspace_graph_create(&amp;graph)</code></td><td>Creates the graph. The caller owns it and eventually calls <code>umi_workspace_graph_destroy(graph)</code>.</td></tr><tr><td><code>umi_workspace_graph_open(graph, root, trusted)</code></td><td>Selects an existing workspace folder. Set trust deliberately; discovering files is not permission to run them.</td></tr><tr><td><code>umi_workspace_graph_discover(graph)</code></td><td>Adds immediate child projects as one complete publication. Existing project IDs and manually added projects remain.</td></tr><tr><td><code>umi_workspace_graph_snapshot(graph, &amp;state)</code></td><td>Copies the root, project count, revision and trust state into caller-owned storage.</td></tr><tr><td><code>umi_workspace_graph_project_at(graph, index, &amp;project)</code></td><td>Copies one project description. Start at index zero and remain below the snapshot count. A concurrent change may require a fresh read.</td></tr><tr><td><code>UMI_STATUS_CAPACITY_EXCEEDED</code></td><td>The configured maximum of 128 projects would be exceeded. No projects from that attempt are published.</td></tr><tr><td>Failed or unchanged discovery</td><td>A failed directory walk does not change the published list or consume its project identifiers. Discovery that finds no additions retains the revision.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Discovery remains synchronous: it finishes its directory walk before returning. It is not an automatic file watcher and does not remove projects whose folders disappeared. Keep the graph alive until the call finishes. Slow storage belongs on an appropriate worker when a host needs to remain responsive.</p>
<h2 id="feedback">2. Show a rejected action without accepting it</h2>
<p>A trading interface contains both business state and presentation state. Business state includes the current environment, order draft and executions. Presentation state includes the explanation shown after an action. A rejection can change the explanation while leaving the order untouched.</p>
<p><code>UmiTradingUiController</code> routes requests through the existing trading workspace. Its changed handler tells the frontend to read a new presentation snapshot. It does <strong>not</strong> mean an order was accepted. Read <code>last_status</code> for the result and <code>last_message</code> for the explanation.</p><pre><code>static void ShowFeedback(uint64_t revision, void *userData)
{
    const UmiTradingUiController *controller = userData;
    UmiTradingUiControllerSnapshot view =
        umi_trading_ui_controller_snapshot(controller);
    (void)revision;
    printf(&quot;%s\n&quot;, view.last_message);
}

/* controller must remain alive while this callback is registered. */
umi_trading_ui_controller_set_changed_handler(
    &amp;controller, ShowFeedback, &amp;controller);</code></pre><p>The snapshot is ready before the callback runs. Rejected live-environment selection, rejected submission, missing selections and invalid draft requests can therefore refresh their explanations just like successful actions. An explicitly timed risk preview uses the same notification path, so one rejection does not cause two notifications.</p>
<p>Keep the callback small. Read state and update a label or model; do not resubmit the order merely because the callback ran. All controller calls and callbacks use the owning thread. Detach the handler before destroying its context or workspace.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Interface</th><th>What to remember</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>umi_trading_ui_controller_init</code></td><td>Initialises caller-owned controller storage with a borrowed workspace. The workspace must outlive its controller use.</td></tr><tr><td><code>umi_trading_ui_controller_set_changed_handler</code></td><td>Borrows the callback and its context. Passing NULL detaches it. Failure feedback is a presentation change too.</td></tr><tr><td><code>umi_trading_ui_controller_snapshot</code></td><td>Returns a copied view of the latest result. Its revision is not a count of accepted trades.</td></tr><tr><td><code>umi_trading_ui_controller_set_environment</code></td><td>Requests an environment through policy. Default configuration rejects live selection and retains simulation.</td></tr><tr><td><code>umi_trading_ui_controller_submit_order</code></td><td>Retains the preview, readiness, live-arming and risk gates. A rejected request is still rejected after the frontend is notified.</td></tr><tr><td><code>UmiTradingUiControllerPreviewOrderAt</code></td><td>Runs the existing risk preview with an explicit time. Its result and notification use the common feedback path.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Malformed calls rejected before accessing a valid controller do not notify a handler. The most recent risk evaluation remains separate from the most recent action result; use the action status when showing a submission error.</p>
<h2 id="launch">3. Keep process ownership until exit is confirmed</h2>
<p>Desk asks a <strong>launcher adapter</strong> to perform operating-system operations. The adapter starts, activates or stops an application. A <strong>process token</strong> identifies the process owned by that adapter; it is not necessarily an operating-system process ID.</p>
<p>Sending a stop request is not the same as confirming exit. If a stop times out or fails, Framework retains the token and changes an otherwise unchanged stopping record to <strong>Attention</strong>. The explanation says that exit is not confirmed. A subsequent Start request cannot quietly create another copy while the existing process is still tracked.</p>
<p>The adapter must return success from Stop only when exit is confirmed. It must not return success simply because it sent a signal. A real host uses its process supervision and exit notifications to establish what actually happened.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Expected behaviour</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Stop failed; no newer lifecycle report</td><td>Retain the process token, report Attention and return the original failure status.</td></tr><tr><td>Restart could not stop the existing process</td><td>Do not start a replacement. Resolve or reconcile the existing process first.</td></tr><tr><td>Exit was reported during the stop callback</td><td>Keep that exit code and explanation. Do not replace them with an assumed successful exit.</td></tr><tr><td>A replacement process was reported during the callback</td><td>Do not clear the replacement token or treat it as the old process. A successful old stop cannot authorise another restart.</td></tr><tr><td>Activation callback is missing</td><td>Return <code>UMI_STATUS_NOT_IMPLEMENTED</code>. Do not mark another application active as though the platform moved focus.</td></tr><tr><td>Availability changed during Restart</td><td>Recheck the start plan before creating a replacement.</td></tr><tr><td>A state message is too long</td><td>Return <code>UMI_STATUS_CAPACITY_EXCEEDED</code> before changing flags, active selection or revisions.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The catalogue belongs to one owning controller thread. Copy snapshots for other readers rather than sharing mutable records. A launcher borrows its catalogue and adapter context; destroy the launcher before those objects expire.</p>
<p>A message can contain at most 511 bytes plus its terminating zero byte. Passing NULL to <code>umi_application_runtime_catalogue_set_state</code> clears the explanation. Updating the catalogue is a state operation, not an operating-system probe.</p>
<h2 id="example">4. Run the example</h2>
<p>The programme below first requests a live environment under the default policy and prints the denial while checking that simulation remains selected. It then uses a controlled process adapter to demonstrate a failed stop followed by a confirmed exit. No broker is contacted and no real process is launched or stopped by this teaching adapter.</p>
<p>From the Applications checkout, configure your usual build and build the example target:</p><pre><code>cmake --build --preset windows-ucrt64-all-debug --target umicom-action-recovery-example --parallel 2
.\build\windows-ucrt64-all-debug\bin\umicom-action-recovery-example.exe</code></pre><p>On Linux, use the corresponding configured build directory:</p><pre><code>cmake --build build/headless-debug --target umicom-action-recovery-example --parallel 2
./build/headless-debug/bin/umicom-action-recovery-example</code></pre><p>The output explains the denied environment, the retained process token and the confirmed stop. The executable returns a nonzero result if those expected states are not observed.</p>
<h3>Complete C source</h3><p>The canonical file is <code>examples/action_recovery/main.c</code>. The Wiki takes this listing from that file when the website is built.</p><pre><code>@UMICOM_SITE_ACTION_RECOVERY_EXAMPLE@</code></pre><h3>How the example is organised</h3>
<p><code>ShowRejectedTradingAction</code> owns the workspace and controller, registers a callback, performs the request and then checks the workspace. The callback only displays feedback. Notice that cleanup detaches it before freeing the workspace.</p>
<p><code>ShowStopRecovery</code> owns a catalogue and launcher. Its adapter context contains a flag that makes the first Stop fail and the second succeed. The important assertion is not whether the callback was called, but whether the catalogue retained the token after failure and removed it after confirmed exit.</p>
<p><code>StartTrainingProcess</code> and <code>StopTrainingProcess</code> are example adapter implementations. Replace them only with a provider that can meet the same contract. In an application, reuse Framework’s existing platform and process services instead of copying this teaching fixture.</p>
<h2 id="source">5. Find the implementation and its tests</h2><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>File or target</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Executable usage</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>include/umicom/platform/workspace_graph.h</code><br><code>src/platform/workspace_graph.c</code></td><td>Workspace/project ownership and complete discovery publication.</td><td><code>tests/action_recovery/test_discovery.c</code>; target <code>umicom-action-recovery-discovery-test</code>.</td></tr><tr><td><code>include/umicom/trading_ui/action_controller.h</code><br><code>src/trading_ui/action_controller.c</code></td><td>Guarded trading requests and presentation feedback.</td><td><code>ShowRejectedTradingAction</code> in the example; <code>tests/action_recovery/test_feedback.c</code>.</td></tr><tr><td><code>include/umicom/application/launcher.h</code><br><code>src/application/launcher.c</code></td><td>Checked launch plans, platform calls and failed-stop handling.</td><td><code>ShowStopRecovery</code> in the example; <code>tests/action_recovery/test_launcher.c</code>.</td></tr><tr><td><code>include/umicom/application/runtime_catalogue.h</code><br><code>src/application/runtime_catalogue.c</code></td><td>Application availability, process ownership, active selection and messages.</td><td><code>tests/action_recovery/test_launcher.c</code>, including message atomicity.</td></tr><tr><td><code>tests/action_recovery/test_discovery_interference.c</code></td><td>Linux completion-boundary and allocation fault fixtures.</td><td>Tests named <code>framework.action_recovery.interference.*</code>.</td></tr><tr><td><code>examples/action_recovery/CMakeLists.txt</code></td><td>Builds the example with the source SDK or an installed <code>UmicomFramework</code> package.</td><td>Links existing <code>Umicom::trading_ui</code> and <code>Umicom::application</code> targets.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h2>6. Check the visible application result</h2>
<p>Use a disposable Notes workspace and simulation data. In Studio, keep a project open while exercising a failed discovery. The previous list should remain. In Trader, request a blocked action and read the explanation without expecting an order. In Desk, use a controlled process fixture to exercise stop failure; do not deliberately stop an application containing unsaved work.</p>
<p>When something differs, open <strong>Report problem</strong>, record the shortest repeatable sequence, add what you expected and export the report. Check the resulting state as well as the screen: a displayed message and an accepted operation are different things. Review notes, logs and screenshots before sharing them.</p>
<p>These recovery rules are shared with future applications. A frontend should display the returned status and preserve the authoritative model rather than implement its own competing recovery policy.</p></main><footer>Umicom Foundation</footer></body></html>
